# Design docs

## Locations

| Document | Role |
| --- | --- |
| `docs/design.ja.md` | Canonical design (Japanese). Contracts, invariants, phases |
| `docs/design-decisions.md` | Rejected alternatives and why (Decision / Reason / Rejected) |
| `docs/adr/NNNN-*.md` | One decision per file whose reason is a measurement (Status, Date, Issue, Context, Decision) |
| `docs/roadmap.md` | Rows: ID, title, Issue, status. No acceptance text |
| `docs/references.md` | Which upstream file to read before a layer is built |
| `docs/conventions.md` | Directory tree, project responsibilities, coding conventions |

`.dev/` is local scratch and is gitignored. Do not put lasting decisions there.

## Changing the design

- Contract, public API, invariant, or phase: open a `design` Issue, update `docs/design.ja.md` (and `docs/design-decisions.md` for what was rejected) in its PR, and merge it before implementation Issues.
- Wording, typos, and examples: may ship in the same PR as code.
- Do not implement first and update the design later. If code and design conflict, stop and fix the design through the process above.

A comparison of approaches in the design includes the approach of each framework that a measurement found fastest, judged by the requirements. In L4 Flutter and Avalonia were the fastest, while §3.1 compared neither Flutter's box constraints nor Avalonia's measure and arrange beyond a reason of structure; the owner's question reopened it (D4, PR #105).

A claim in the design about what another implementation has or does (a library version, a framework's widget, a browser) names the file and line, or the documentation page, where it was read, and is read there before it goes in. In L3 the Grill wrote that Taffy 0.15 has sizing keywords in min and max; its `min_size` and `max_size` are `LengthPercentageAuto` (`style/mod.rs` lines 757 and 760), which the implementation found (PR #134). It was the third such error after Flutter's `Offstage` and Taffy's containing block in L2.

When the version of a compared framework changes, the claims that cite the old version are checked again in the new one. In L9 the design cited "the Taffy 0.9.0 that GPUI uses" seven times; Zed ships 0.13.0, where two of the claims (no sizing keywords, no `safe` alignment) no longer hold (PR #140).

A design PR that changes a decision searches the whole design for the sections that restate it, and changes them in the same PR. In F1 the PR changed §4.5's bundled font, and §8.12, §4.6, §11's test mode, §4.1's diagram, and the table of failures still assumed the old one; the review found the five (PR #146).

Every type in a signature the design writes is defined in the design or the code. F1's first `IFontProvider` took `in FontStyle style`, which nothing defined, so whether it carried the language could not be read (PR #146).

A contract that hands work to an OS API is written after reading that API's output parameters, not only its inputs: what it returns when it finds nothing, and what else it returns. F1's first interface dropped `MapCharacters`'s `scale` and left the length of a range that no font renders undefined; the documentation defines both (PR #146).

A design that changes what a layout or render algorithm reads checks every cache and boundary rule against the change, the path that hits a cache included. In L1 a review found that "a child whose size the parent decided is a boundary" does not hold with the automatic minimum size, wrapping, and baseline alignment, and the re-review found that a mark set again in each run was lost in a run that hit the cache (PR #75, Issue #76).

A design that stops a propagation with a comparison decides two things in the design itself: everything the propagation carried, and what happens when a value to compare is gone. In L5 both came from reviews: the measurement cache (4 entries) can overwrite a value the parent read, and the path's `SubtreeDirty` carried Paint, HitTest, and Text with Layout, so stopping at the parent left the repaint with no carrier (PR #109).

A sentence added to the design to answer a review is a claim like any other and is checked before it goes in. The L5 re-review explained that a leaf an ancestor lays out first has "nothing to compare", and the design took that over; the parent's measurement cache still held a value of the old content, and the random test with several changes per pass showed the wrong rectangle (PR #109, #115).

After a script edits a Markdown table, read `git diff --stat`. A diff of the whole table means its columns were padded again (it happened in S9 to `docs/roadmap.md`, by no step of the script): restore the file and replace only the rows that change.

## Judge the current optimum

The design records a decision made at that time. It is not proof that the decision is still the best one.

- Recommend the best solution for the problem as it stands now.
- Do not cite an existing sentence as the reason to keep that shape.
- Do not add public API only so an older shape can stay.
- If the written decision is no longer the best one, say so and change the design through the process above.
